WHAT IS IPC
Inter-Process Communication (IPC) is a mechanism that allows independent processes to exchange data and synchronize their actions.

DEFINITION OF IPC
IPC is a set of mechanisms provided by the Operating System that enables processes running in separate memory spaces to communicate with each other, share data, and coordinate their execution.
In simple terms, processes cannot directly access each other's memory, so IPC acts as a bridge that allows them to communicate.

CHARACTERISTICS OF IPC
• Data Exchange: Allows processes to send and receive data.
• Synchronization: Helps processes coordinate their actions.
• Isolation Support: Works even though processes have separate memory spaces.
• OS-Managed: The Operating System provides and controls the communication channels.
• Flexible: Can be used for communication between processes on the same computer or across a network.

WHY IPC IS NEEDED
• Processes are isolated by design for security and stability, so they need a way to share data.
• Modern applications are divided into multiple processes that need to work together.
• Without IPC, processes cannot easily communicate or cooperate to complete complex tasks.
• IPC allows processes to share information and system resources instead of duplicating work.
• It helps different processes coordinate their activities.
Example:
A web browser and a web server need to exchange requests and responses. IPC provides mechanisms that allow processes to communicate and coordinate with each other.

METHODS OF IPC
1) PIPES
A Pipe is one of the simplest and oldest forms of IPC. It provides a communication channel through which data can flow from one process to another.
A pipe is usually unidirectional, meaning data flows in only one direction. One process writes data into the pipe, and another process reads that data.

A pipe has two ends:
• Write end – used to put data into the pipe.
• Read end – used to take data from the pipe.

The pipe works like a physical water pipe. One process puts data into one end, and the other process receives the data from the other end.
The pipe uses a temporary buffer in memory to hold the data until the receiving process reads it.

HOW PIPES WORK
First, the Operating System creates a pipe with a read end and a write end. The writing process sends data through the write end. The data is temporarily stored in the pipe. The receiving process then reads the data from the read end.
Pipes are generally used between related processes, such as a parent process and its child process.

KEY POINTS 
• Pipes are mainly unidirectional.
• Data flows from the write end to the read end.
• They are generally used between related processes.
• They do not have a permanent name in the filesystem.
• They are temporary and exist while the processes are using them.
• They provide a simple and fast method of IPC.
• The Operating System manages the pipe and its buffer.
• Data is read in the order in which it was written.

EXAMPLE OF A PIPE
In a Linux terminal:
ls -l | grep ".txt"

Here:
• ls -l produces a list of files.
• The | symbol represents the pipe.
• grep ".txt" receives the output of ls.
• grep filters the output and displays files containing .txt.
So, the pipe allows the output of one process to become the input of another process.

ADVANTAGES OF PIPES
• Simple to use.
• Fast communication between processes.
• Managed by the Operating System.
• Useful for transferring data between related processes.
• Does not require a separate file for communication.

2) NAMED PIPES (FIFOs)
A Named Pipe, also called a FIFO (First In, First Out), is an advanced form of a normal pipe.
The main difference is that a named pipe has a name in the filesystem. Because of this name, processes can find and use the pipe even if they are unrelated processes.
A named pipe behaves like a normal pipe for transferring data, but its name makes it easier for different processes to communicate.

HOW NAMED PIPES WORK
A named pipe is first created as a special file using a command such as:mkfifo mypipe
After the named pipe is created, one process can write data into it, while another process can read the data from it.
The processes do not need to have a parent-child relationship. They only need to know the name or location of the named pipe.
The data follows FIFO order, meaning the data that enters first is read first.

EXAMPLE
Suppose a logging process writes messages into:/tmp/logfifo
Another monitoring process can read those messages from the same named pipe.
The two processes can be completely unrelated. The name of the pipe allows them to communicate.

KEY POINTS OF NAMED PIPES
• Also called FIFO.
• Has a name in the filesystem.
• Can be used by unrelated processes.
• Usually provides one-way communication.
• Data is transferred in First In, First Out order.
• The pipe can be accessed by processes that know its name.
• More flexible than an ordinary pipe.
• Useful when processes need to communicate even though they were created separately.

ADVANTAGES OF NAMED PIPES
• Can communicate between unrelated processes.
• Has a name, so processes can easily identify the communication channel.
• Simple to use.
• Useful for continuous communication between independent programs.
• Maintains FIFO order of data.

DIFFERENCE BETWEEN PIPES AND NAMED PIPES
Pipes:
• Usually used between related processes.
• Does not have a name in the filesystem.
• Temporary communication channel.
• Simple and less flexible.
• Usually one-way.
• Created and managed by the Operating System.
• Example: ls -l | grep ".txt"

Named Pipes (FIFOs):
• Can be used between unrelated processes.
• Has a name in the filesystem.
• Has a filesystem entry.
• More flexible.
• Usually one-way.
• Created as a special named file.
• Example: /tmp/myfifo


